              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Grid Patterns**\n\nOkay, I'm now focusing on the composition of grid inputs, specifically identifying separators and colored cell arrangements. Initial analysis involves examining training data, such as Train 1. I'm noting separator positions (0s) and the row-wise placement of colored cells. Further analysis will concentrate on deriving patterns or rules.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Color Frequencies**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. Analyzing those frequency counts, it looks like I must be excluding the most frequent value (which could be a background or a separator). The output appears to be the remaining values, sorted in descending order of their frequency count. I confirmed the hypothesis by testing with Train 2; the most frequent color is omitted, and the other values are presented in the correct order.\n\n\n**Confirming Block Frequencies**\n\nI've just been looking at Train 3, and I have found what appears to be a generalizable rule. After extracting and counting each block value, I can see that the output is composed of the remaining values in descending order of frequency, excluding the separator. My prior assumptions about shape and dimension held true! Train 4 confirms this, as well.\n\n\n",
